# 03 — Data sources

No raw source data is committed. `data/` is gitignored; a release is
reproduced by running the ingest against locally downloaded sources, which
keeps redistribution questions out of the repository entirely.

Every edge carries `primary_knowledge_source` as an infores CURIE, so
attribution is queryable **per fact** rather than stated in aggregate. Any
publication derived from this graph should cite
`SELECT DISTINCT primary_knowledge_source` over the subgraph it used, not the
whole register.

## What each layer read, and what version

{{layer_table}}

**{{layers_no_version}}** declare no source version. That is the gap, not a
formatting detail: `kgav.provenance.write_source` exists and those layers do
not call it, so no figure derived from them can be traced to a database
release. Guide to PHARMACOLOGY is the one source that states its release
inside the file, and its `published` date is the only release date in this
project that is not a download timestamp.

## The licence position

The code is MIT. The data is not, the terms differ materially, and one of them
prohibits commercial use outright.

**DrugBank is excluded by decision, not by oversight.** Its academic download
is CC BY-NC. Once its identifiers are baked into a release, removing them
later means rebuilding rather than patching. The `drugbank_id` property exists
in the schema so the field is there if a licence is ever obtained; it stays
null. ChEMBL (CC BY-SA 3.0) constrains how derived releases may be
*redistributed*, not whether they may be used.

`DATA_LICENSES.md` holds the register, with a `verified` column to be filled
from each download page at download time — not from the table, and not from a
paper citing the source. Two questions are open and belong at the download
page: Guide to PHARMACOLOGY required registration when checked, and the same
page stated that commercial organisations pay an access fee. Whether that
constrains *use* or only *access* is exactly the distinction the DrugBank
decision turned on.

## The sources, and what each one is for

### UniProt — the spine
Reviewed proteomes give `Protein` and `Gene` nodes; UniProt's own Reactome and
GO cross-references give `Pathway` nodes and `PARTICIPATES_IN`. The register
names, per virus, the exact accession and taxon whose `PRO_` chains are used,
because UniProt's taxonomy search includes descendants and a lineage-walking
lookup leaves no record of which strain it picked.

### STRING v12.0 — the host interactome
The filter is **`experimental >= 400`**, not the conventional
`combined_score >= 700`, and the reason changes every downstream path count.
On the v12.0 human file the conventional cut keeps 474k edges of which 284k
(60%) have `experimental < 400` — they clear the bar on text-mining and
coexpression — while 120k edges with strong experimental support fall below it
and would be discarded. Text-mining co-occurrence measures how often two
proteins are written about together, which tracks study bias, not binding; for
a network-proximity baseline, where the whole quantity of interest is distance
through the interactome, that inflates closeness around well-studied proteins.
Every channel score is kept as an edge qualifier, so the `combined >= 700`
variant is reconstructible by query rather than re-ingest.

### VirHostNet and IntAct — the virus-host bridge
The only bridge between the viral spine and the host interactome, so M3 exists
entirely because of it. The SARS-CoV-2 detection-method histogram is dominated
by proximity-dependent biotin identification and affinity capture — roughly
6,600 of ~7,000 rows. BioID labels anything within about 10 nm, so those edges
mean *in the same complex neighbourhood*, not *binds*. The distinction is
preserved rather than averaged: `detection_method`, `interaction_type`
(MI:0407 direct vs MI:0915 co-complex) and `source_score` all travel on the
edge. All of them are evidence tier 1, because tier records **how a fact was
established**, not how good the assay was.

### BioGRID ORCS — CRISPR host factors
The densest repurposing signal in the graph, and the only layer where getting
a sign backwards produces a *confidently wrong* recommendation rather than a
missing one:

```
dependency factor knocked out  -> virus cannot replicate -> cell survives
restriction factor knocked out -> virus replicates better -> cell dies
```

Both register as "hit". Topologically identical, biologically opposite.
Direction is determined in order of confidence: a one-sided
`SCREEN_RATIONALE` (41 of 96 coronavirus screens); otherwise the sign of a
signed per-gene effect, calibrated per screen because ORCS screens do not
agree on a sign convention — 21 use one and 11 the other. `effect_size` on the
edge is **signed under one convention** (positive means dependency); the
number the source published travels separately as `source_score`. Before that
calibration, 1,653 of 11,112 edges had a direction contradicting the sign of
their own effect size.

### Curated screens — the flavivirus gap

**This layer is declared and is not in this release.**
{{layers_absent_why}}, so the flavivirus host factors it exists to supply are
absent from the graph described here. **{{n_taxa_without_host_factors}} of the
{{n_viruses_built}} viruses carry no `HOST_FACTOR_FOR` edge at all**
({{taxa_without_host_factors}}) and therefore have no dependency-factor set
for the pathway landscape to overlap — five of those are exactly the
flaviviruses this layer was written for, and the other three are uncovered for
reasons of their own. The rest of this section describes what the layer does
when its table is populated.

{{host_factor_table}}

ORCS carries nothing for DENV-1, DENV-3, DENV-4, JEV or TBEV. Surveying the
literature returns screens for DENV, ZIKV, WNV and YFV and **none** for JEV or
TBEV, so for two of nine surveyed flaviviruses the data does not exist at
genome scale anywhere. For the rest it exists in supplementary tables, which
is a curation problem rather than an engineering one — so this layer reads a
table a human filled in, one row per hit, and emits the same
`HOST_FACTOR_FOR` edges. A row with no direction is **refused**, never
defaulted.

### ChEMBL 37 — chemistry
Four edge types: `INHIBITS` and `MEASURED_INACTIVE_AGAINST` against viral
proteins, `TARGETS` against host proteins, and
`HAS_ANTIVIRAL_ACTIVITY_AGAINST` against the organism. **A viral-protein row
becomes `INHIBITS` or `MEASURED_INACTIVE_AGAINST` according to
`labels.classify`** — the same function that decides the labels, so the
features and the labels cannot disagree about what "acts on" means.

Censored values are preserved: a row reading `IC50 > 10000 nM` is a negative
result, and coercing it to a number inverts its meaning. 10,766 of 17,366
coronavirus IC50 records are censored, so this is not an edge case.

### The selectivity layer — correcting an earlier error
This project previously recorded that ChEMBL contains almost no cytotoxicity
data for coronaviruses: 2 CC50 records across 21,900 activities. **That was
wrong, and the error was in the query rather than the database** —
cytotoxicity assays are filed against the *cell line*, not the virus. ChEMBL 37
holds 74,450 CC50 records under cell-line targets.

Pairing is **same-document only**, because CC50/EC50 is interpretable only if
both numbers come from one experimental context. A CC50 in HeLa and an EC50 in
Calu-3 produce a number with the right units and no meaning. Compounds without
a same-document pair are marked `selectivity_verified: false` rather than
given a fabricated ratio.

### NCATS OpenData — the plate-matched population
The layer that exists because two of the measured confounds are properties of
the **label set**, not of the graph: ChEMBL negatives are antiviral research
compounds that carry viral-target annotations and almost no host ones (1.5% of
measured-inactive compounds reach a dependency factor against 12.1%
expected), and {{lo_share_pct}} of compounds carrying both an `INHIBITS` edge
and an antiviral label cite the same PMID on both — {{lo_share_a_pub}} of
{{lo_both}} — because one paper reports the target IC50 and the cell-based
EC50 together.

The panel reports what did **not** work — 9,848 of 10,633 rows are curve class
4, tested and inactive — from approved and annotated libraries, and is
generated independently of ChEMBL target annotation, so
`--publication-disjoint` withholds nothing when scoring against it. Its CPE
and pseudotyped-entry assays are kept as **separate** `assay_type` values,
registered in `docs/prereg_ncats_entry_assays.md`: a pseudotyped particle
carries the named virus's spike on another viral core, so inhibiting its entry
is evidence about entry and not about replication of the authentic virus.
Pooling them would destroy the only thing a plate-matched cell is worth.

### Guide to PHARMACOLOGY — direction
Before this layer, 12,155 of 14,381 `TARGETS` edges stated no direction
(84.5%). On the assembled release, which contains it, 10,177 of 17,635 state
none — 57.8% — and the directed count rose 2,226 → 7,587 by two routes: new
directed edges, and existing ChEMBL edges gaining an action through the
qualifier ladder.

### ChEMBL indications — the second label axis
`TREATS` and `IN_TRIAL_FOR` are declared evaluation labels and carried **zero
edges on every release** until this layer, so every AUC in the repository
rested on one predicate derived from in-vitro concentration-response at one
threshold. This layer is forbidden by its own registration from adding a
`SmallMolecule`: a new node would change every other population in the
repository and make `make auc-delta` uninterpretable for the release that
introduced the label meant to be an independent check.

### Chemical similarity — the cold start
953 of 1,846 SARS-CoV-2 positives have no metapath at all, and 947 of those
have no target edges whatsoever. `CHEMICALLY_SIMILAR_TO` is the only route
that can reach them, by letting a compound borrow a structural neighbour's
annotations. Scope is **bipartite on purpose** — only (path-less → annotated)
pairs — because all-pairs over 12,821 compounds is 82M comparisons and most of
the resulting edges would add degree without adding reach. What it cannot fix:
similarity inherits a neighbour's artifacts wholesale, so an analogue of
chloroquine inherits the SIGMAR1 route.

### InterPro / folds — the cross-family route
Phase 0 measured, at chain level, that all three protease families share a
fold and that **the plan's assumption that they differ was wrong**. What
separates them is the nucleophile, read from the sequence at UniProt's
annotated active site. The table that used to be here was wrong and it was the
project's foundational measurement: printing every shared class instead of the
first two showed SSF50494 on MERS-CoV and HCoV-229E 3CLpro and on all five 3Cs
and all six NS3pros — but **not** on SARS-CoV-2 or SARS-CoV 3CLpro, the two
chains the project exists to serve.
